AquaStream360 - Contamination Source Investigator

________________________________________
What This Application Does
Contamination Investigator is an analysis tool in the water quality investigator in AquaStream360 that helps you trace contamination through water distribution networks. It allows you to:
•	Predict how contamination spreads from entry points
•	Find where contamination came from based on detection points
•	Analyze multiple sources simultaneously for complex contamination events
The application uses simulation to model water quality and trace contaminant pathways through your network.
________________________________________
Main Interface Overview
The interface is split into two main sections:
Left Panel - Controls
Contains all settings, mode selection, and analysis controls:
•	Analysis Mode dropdown - Choose what you want to analyze
•	Find Sources Settings - Configure nodes and timesteps
•	Detection Points list - Manage multiple points
•	RUN ANALYSIS button - Execute the analysis
•	Export Table Data - Save results to CSV/Excel/JSON
•	Show/Hide Data Table - Toggle results visibility
Right Panel - Visualization
•	Network Plot - Interactive map of your water network
•	Results Table - Detailed numerical results (toggle on/off)
________________________________________
Analysis Modes
Mode 1: Forward Trace (Predict Spread)
Predict how contamination spreads from a single entry point.
When to use:
You know where contamination entered and want to see which areas will be affected over time.
How to use:
1.	Select "Forward Trace (Predict spread)" from the dropdown
2.	Choose the Entry Node from the dropdown or click a node on the plot
3.	Set the Analysis Timestep to see contamination at different times
4.	Click RUN ANALYSIS
What you'll see:
•	Green marker: Your entry point
•	Red pipes: Contaminated pipes at selected timestep
•	Table: List of affected nodes with trace percentages
________________________________________
Mode 2: Forward Trace From Multiple Entry Points
Analyze combined impact from several contamination sources.
When to use:
Multiple contamination entry points exist, and you want to see their combined effect.
How to use:
1.	Select "Forward Trace From Multiple Entry Points"
2.	Add entry points by clicking nodes on the plot (or use the Add button)
o	Click a node once → adds to entry points (green marker appears)
o	Click the same node again → removes it
3.	Add at least 2 entry points
4.	Set the Analysis Timestep
5.	Click RUN ANALYSIS
What you'll see:
•	Green markers: All entry points
•	Red pipes: Combined contaminated network
•	Table: Each node's average influence from all entry points
________________________________________
Mode 3: Find Entry Points (Trace Backwards)
Identify possible contamination sources based on a detection.
When to use:
You detected contamination at a specific node and want to find where it might have come from.
How to use:
1.	Select "Find Entry Points (Trace backwards)"
2.	Choose the Detection Node where contamination was found
3.	Set the Detection Timestep (when it was detected)
4.	Check/uncheck Advanced Investigation:
o	ON (default): Runs detailed contribution analysis for each possible source
o	OFF: Shows only which nodes are upstream
5.	Click RUN ANALYSIS
What you'll see:
•	Red marker: Your detection point
•	Green pipes: All possible upstream paths
•	With Advanced ON: Contribution percentages for each possible source
•	With Advanced OFF: List of upstream nodes
________________________________________
Mode 4: Multi-Source Inversion
Analyze multiple detection points to identify common sources.
When to use:
Contamination detected at several locations, and you want to find sources that could explain all detections.
How to use:
1.	Select "Multi-Source Inversion (Multiple detections)"
2.	Add detection points by clicking nodes on the plot
o	Click to add, click again to remove
o	Each detection point appears in the list
3.	Add at least 2 detection points
4.	Set the Detection Timestep (same for all points)
5.	Click RUN ANALYSIS
What you'll see:
•	Red markers: All detection points
•	Green pipes: Upstream network common to all detections
•	Table: Ranked list of possible sources by frequency of appearance
